00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:46.810
it is very difficult
to build new versions,

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:49.610
and push new releases
of this application.

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:52.060
So it ended up running
for a long time.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.040
And while it's running
different roles

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.670
from different part
of the production,

00:02:55.670 --> 00:03:00.700
or from the organization,
needs to do things just running

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:01.930
application.

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:05.580
For example, security
engineers may come and patch,

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:09.930
apply updates, when
things becomes outdated.

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:12.490
Admins may have to come
and restart the software

00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:14.470
when it gets stuck.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:17.420
Software engineers may have
to come and manually make

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:21.110
adjustment, because when new
requirement get published,

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:22.390
things change.

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:24.460
Before you know it,
you have a number

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:26.950
of people, and a
number of paths,

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:30.790
that can apply changes
to production software.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:34.420
With this fairly
broad attack surface,

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:38.300
a attacker, or rogue
employee, could come by,

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:41.630
and slip in a piece of bad
code to one of the changes

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:43.182
to the production software.

00:03:43.182 --> 00:03:44.890
Now, voila, you have
a security incident.

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:49.240
So you may think
that, well, that

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.790
is legacy software for you.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:55.330
I plan to update all of my
production infrastructure

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:57.000
to use containers.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.870
I am good, right?

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:01.320
Take a look.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:05.660
So, this user has a container
running in production.

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:08.870
It is true that containers
refine the software supply

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:10.250
chain.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:11.720
You don't have a
number of people

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:14.790
apply changes to the
software directly anymore.

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:21.050
Instead, developers have to
rebuild and repush a container,

00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:23.390
in order to apply any changes
to it, because containers

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:24.630
are immutable.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:28.670
So now organizations have
a centralized chokepoint,

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:33.230
where all changes to production
deployments are applied,

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:35.330
and a lot of them
take advantage of that

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:40.220
to bake in streamlined
security controls,

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:44.750
and test to make sure that
all the production changes are

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:46.070
up to snuff.

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:49.640
Things like making sure it's
build from trusted sources,

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:51.860
making sure it passes
all the unit tests,

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:54.110
making sure it clears
vulnerability scans,

00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:56.150
making sure analysis--

00:04:56.150 --> 00:04:58.970
static analysis don't find
any fault with the code,

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:02.660
and it has been manually OK'd
by a quality control engineer.

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:03.950
It is all good.

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:06.080
But it still does
not fundamentally

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:09.200
change how deployments
are controlled.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.510
It is still a count-based
deployment control,

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:16.360
which means that if you have
an employee, or attacker who

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:17.960
stole the credential
of an employee,

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:20.690
have access to push
code in production.

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:24.980
They could still just bypass
all of those streamlined CI/CD

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:29.660
controls, and push a untrusted
piece of code in production

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:31.740
directly.

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:36.090
Now, that is where binary
authorization comes in play.

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:38.900
Binary authorization makes
sure that only properly

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:43.460
signed containers are deployed
to the Kubernetes Engine.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:47.270
It gives you a tool to
define policies around

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:51.260
what can be deployed, in
addition to who can deploy.

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:56.270
Addressing the weakness in a
count-based deployed control

00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:58.170
mechanism.

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:00.980
So let's take a look
at how it works.

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:04.160
Users have a piece
of code that-- he

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:07.160
has a CI/CD pipeline
set up, run the code

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:10.430
through a set of required
tests and controls

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:13.120
before pushing to production.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:18.020
Binary authorization integrates
with the CI/CD pipeline

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:21.920
by having them producing
a signature as the image

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:26.060
passes through the individual
stages in the pipeline.

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:29.700
So a builder would sign
a container that says,

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:31.280
I'm the trusted builder.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:33.502
I am the one who
built the container.

00:06:33.502 --> 00:06:34.960
Tester will sign
the container that

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:38.690
says this container
passed my test pipeline.

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:40.920
Vulnerability sign
the container when

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:45.210
it doesn't find any known
existing vulnerabilities on it.

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:47.360
Analysis you know.

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:52.040
In QA, engineers put their
respective signatures

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:54.330
on this piece of image.

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:58.790
So by the time the image comes
to be deployed to production,

00:06:58.790 --> 00:07:02.750
binary authorization is
integrated into the GKE deploy

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:08.000
API, that will look at the
signatures that are produced

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:10.580
by the different stages
in the CI/CD pipeline,

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:14.040
compare that against
customer-defined policies

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:17.000
on what can deploy
in production,

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:21.150
and then make a deploy decision.

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:24.050
So now, if you have a
employee with access

00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:26.930
to push code to production
comes along to push them

00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:30.680
untrusted code, it would fail
because the image would not

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:33.980
have the required
signatures on it.

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:38.670
Now you have code-based
deployment controls.

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:41.780
So, at a high level,
this is how it works,

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:47.680
and we've worked with design
partners, such as Shopify,

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:49.250
to make sure that
it can properly

00:07:49.250 --> 00:07:52.580
integrate into your
existing production set up.

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:54.560
We have a number of
features that I'm

00:07:54.560 --> 00:08:00.200
going to go through with you
today, to see how you may--

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:04.800
to see how it works with
your existing infrastructure.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:07.370
So first of all, you
probably already have--

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:09.120
if you use container,
you probably already

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:11.040
have a CI/CD pipeline setup.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:15.300
You want to make sure
that your process can

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:19.630
be re-used across a large
number of production deployment.

00:08:19.630 --> 00:08:21.480
You don't want to a
separate repository,

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:24.030
a separate pipeline, for each
environmental application

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:25.150
that you build.

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:28.740
Binary authorization
accommodates that by

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:32.700
defined policies at the
runtime environment level.

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:34.610
What do I mean by that?

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:38.070
Let's say you have two clusters
on GKE, a prod and a dev.

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:39.559
For the production
environment, you

00:08:39.559 --> 00:08:41.640
want to make sure
that an image has

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:46.380
to be signed off by all the
required CI/CD stages before it

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:50.280
can run, because it has
access to live customer data.

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:52.470
But for a dev
environment, you want

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:54.780
to maintain developer velocity.

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:59.280
So as long as image has
been built properly,

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:02.370
has passed the unit
test, it's good to go.

00:09:02.370 --> 00:09:04.650
There are difference
in deployed time

00:09:04.650 --> 00:09:07.890
policy for this
environment, but they still

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:10.770
use the same CI/CD set up.

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:13.740
For a production
deployment, it has

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:16.530
to go through the
entire CI/CD pipeline,

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:19.440
clear all stages before
it can be deployed,

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:25.950
but for the dev environment, you
have the options of build it,

00:09:25.950 --> 00:09:29.690
test it, take it
out of the pipeline,

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:31.680
deploy it then and there.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:34.691
Or, if after running in
production-- in dev environment

00:09:34.691 --> 00:09:36.690
for a while, you're like
this container is good,

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:38.273
I'm going to promote
it to production,

00:09:38.273 --> 00:09:41.860
you don't have to duplicate
any of your CI/CD set up.

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:45.840
You can just push it through
the remaining required tests,

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:48.900
and now it has all the
required signatures.

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:51.510
The same image that
was deployed to dev

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:53.910
can now be deployed
to production.

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:57.810
So you can maintain
a centralized, shared

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:00.610
CI/CD process across
all [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:07.310
environments, which is easier
to manage and more secure.

00:10:07.310 --> 00:10:11.880
Binary authorization integrates
with the centralized metadata

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:15.750
store hosted by Google
Container Registry.

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:17.940
It's called Container
Analysis API.

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:21.420
We've also announced
this product today.

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:24.030
Container Analysis
API is designed

00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:28.260
to be a one-stop shop for
all metadata associated

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:29.890
with a container.

00:10:29.890 --> 00:10:33.500
So that a centralized
stakeholder can,

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:36.220
if he has questions about
a particular container that

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:38.650
is being deployed
or that is running,

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:41.372
he can go in and check
to see, you know,

00:10:41.372 --> 00:10:42.580
what are all the information?

00:10:42.580 --> 00:10:45.210
What are all the things I
know about the container?

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:48.640
So as a container
goes through its

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:51.490
build processes, for example,
the builder would generate

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:54.780
a build record indicating,
what are the sources

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:56.350
used for this container?

00:10:56.350 --> 00:10:59.140
What are the packages
of what's included?

00:10:59.140 --> 00:11:00.520
When was it built?

00:11:00.520 --> 00:11:01.800
Will built it?

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:04.090
Container goes through test.

00:11:04.090 --> 00:11:07.560
The tester can write a
standardized metadata

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:09.960
about the test result.
It goes through scanner,

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:13.540
standardized metadata around
vulnerability findings

00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:16.730
for the image.

00:11:16.730 --> 00:11:20.470
So by the time the
image comes to deploy,

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:22.660
controls such as
binary authorization

00:11:22.660 --> 00:11:24.880
can take a look at
all the metadata that

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:27.610
are produced in the
Container Analysis API,

00:11:27.610 --> 00:11:29.710
and apply policies,
apply controls,

00:11:29.710 --> 00:11:33.230
to determine what can
deploy in production.

00:11:33.230 --> 00:11:36.280
So binary authorization's
signature format

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:39.585
is just one of the metadata
that Container Analysis API

00:11:39.585 --> 00:11:40.085
supports.

00:11:42.610 --> 00:11:46.840
The Google engineers have
also published a open source

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:52.210
standard called Grafeas for
this container metadata analysis

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:53.420
API.

00:11:53.420 --> 00:11:57.970
It uses the same metadata format
and uses the same API format,

00:11:57.970 --> 00:12:00.170
so that you can collect,
and produce and collect

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:04.946
this information for
your on-prem environment.

00:12:04.946 --> 00:12:08.410
Binary authorization also
published a Kritis open source

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:13.570
project, which is a open source
implementation of deploy time

00:12:13.570 --> 00:12:15.790
enforcement with Kubernetes--
very similar to binary

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:17.260
authorization--

00:12:17.260 --> 00:12:19.600
which, combined
with Grafeas, you

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:23.110
have the building blocks to
implement a similar enforcement

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:26.710
flow for your
on-prem deployment,

00:12:26.710 --> 00:12:29.420
similar to what we have
here for Kubernetes Engine.

00:12:34.850 --> 00:12:40.950
If you use Google GCP tools,
such as Google Container

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:46.260
Builder and Google Container
Registry Vulnerability Scanner,

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:47.880
there are a set
of metadata that's

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:49.950
already produced for you.

00:12:49.950 --> 00:12:53.610
So if your code is viewed
by Google Container Build,

00:12:53.610 --> 00:12:57.360
it will already produce
a verifiable build record

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:00.020
for you, for every
container that it builds,

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:01.590
and today that is happening.

00:13:01.590 --> 00:13:04.210
It is stored in the
Container Analysis API.

00:13:04.210 --> 00:13:08.310
Similarly, when you push a image
into Google Container Registry,

00:13:08.310 --> 00:13:10.220
it gets scanned by
the vul scanner, which

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:14.020
was also announced today, it
will publish a vulnerability

00:13:14.020 --> 00:13:16.350
findings.

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:18.140
Now, while the most
common use cases

00:13:18.140 --> 00:13:20.450
that we hear from
customers is, I

00:13:20.450 --> 00:13:22.730
want to be able to
gauge deployment

00:13:22.730 --> 00:13:26.420
based on build and
vulnerability information,

00:13:26.420 --> 00:13:29.210
the tricky thing with that
is, every organization

00:13:29.210 --> 00:13:33.040
has a different definition of
what is acceptable in a build,

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:35.480
and in a vulnerability findings.

00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:39.800
That is why we give you a tool,
a open source signer, that you

00:13:39.800 --> 00:13:45.810
can use to define what your
organization's requirements are

00:13:45.810 --> 00:13:50.630
to sign a build and a
vulnerability record.

00:13:50.630 --> 00:13:55.790
So the way it works is, the
user would take the signer,

00:13:55.790 --> 00:13:59.060
apply your own custom
configuration that says things

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:06.540
like, in a build record, I
will only sign a container

00:14:06.540 --> 00:14:09.140
to be OK to deploy
if the source comes

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:11.720
from these following
three repositories.

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:14.510
Or, I would only
sign a container

00:14:14.510 --> 00:14:21.290
to be state ready if it does not
contain critical vulnerability

00:14:21.290 --> 00:14:23.580
findings.

00:14:23.580 --> 00:14:25.890
And when that
happens, the signer

00:14:25.890 --> 00:14:30.210
issues the attestation, using
a customer-provided key.

00:14:30.210 --> 00:14:33.190
By the time the container
reaches production,

00:14:33.190 --> 00:14:35.640
binary authorization will
look up that attestation--

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:38.780
because the user would put
that in the policy to say,

00:14:38.780 --> 00:14:40.970
I will require
this attestation--

00:14:40.970 --> 00:14:45.150
and enforce, to make sure
only images with proper

00:14:45.150 --> 00:14:49.720
build and vulnerability
finding records can deploy

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:52.980
to the production environment.

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:56.160
We also understand--
so far we told you

00:14:56.160 --> 00:15:01.350
about how only properly signed
images that are built in-house

00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:03.600
can be deployed to
your environment.

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:07.560
But we also recognize
that not all images people

00:15:07.560 --> 00:15:10.440
run on their production
environment are built in-house.

00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:15.120
There are popular third party
images, such as Nginx and Redis

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:18.390
that a lot of customers deploy.

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:21.450
But it's also a common
source of vulnerabilities

00:15:21.450 --> 00:15:25.350
in many enterprise environment.

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:29.970
So to address that issue,
we support image whitelist

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:32.370
in binary authorization policy.

00:15:32.370 --> 00:15:35.230
So a security
stakeholder could say,

00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:40.220
I want all of the Nginx
instances deployed

00:15:40.220 --> 00:15:46.020
in my organization to be the
up-to-date, vulnerability free

00:15:46.020 --> 00:15:48.780
one, that I deemed OK.

00:15:48.780 --> 00:15:50.950
So he would put
that in the policy.

00:15:50.950 --> 00:15:52.440
And when a employee
comes and try

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:57.120
to push the latest secure Nginx
image, the digest will match,

00:15:57.120 --> 00:15:59.920
and the deployment
will go through.

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:03.150
But if somebody tries to
push the outdated, vulnerable

00:16:03.150 --> 00:16:06.160
version of the same
third party image,

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:08.160
it would fail, and
generate the audit log,

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:13.160
so the security team can review
these incidents later on,

00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:17.555
and therefore secure your
deployment environment.

00:16:17.555 --> 00:16:21.510
Binary authorization integrates
with Kubernetes master

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:24.210
to make sure it apply
verification, apply

00:16:24.210 --> 00:16:28.440
the policies, to every
single deployment that goes

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:30.820
to your production project.

00:16:30.820 --> 00:16:33.620
So when a deployer comes along--
the deployer could be a human,

00:16:33.620 --> 00:16:36.810
could be a automated
pipeline, does not matter--

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:40.340
it sends a request to
Kubernetes master that says,

00:16:40.340 --> 00:16:44.530
I am deploying digest
foo to cluster bar, which

00:16:44.530 --> 00:16:47.170
then would forward this
request to the admissions

00:16:47.170 --> 00:16:52.150
controller, which is part
of the Kubernetes master.

00:16:52.150 --> 00:16:55.600
If this project or this cluster
has binary authorization turned

00:16:55.600 --> 00:16:59.290
on, admissions controller
would forward this request

00:16:59.290 --> 00:17:01.400
to binary authorization,
which then would go

00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:05.339
look up all the signatures
associated with it,

00:17:05.339 --> 00:17:08.589
come back, verify
those signatures using

00:17:08.589 --> 00:17:11.410
public keys defined
in the policy,

00:17:11.410 --> 00:17:15.760
and return a
verification decision.

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:18.819
Once Kubernetes master
receives a decision,

00:17:18.819 --> 00:17:23.747
it can then go ahead--
either block or allow it

00:17:23.747 --> 00:17:26.550
to deployment.

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:30.410
But we understand that
production emergencies

00:17:30.410 --> 00:17:31.520
do happen.

00:17:31.520 --> 00:17:33.290
What if your things
are on fire, so I want

00:17:33.290 --> 00:17:35.560
to push a nonconformant change?

00:17:35.560 --> 00:17:37.280
We allow that.

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:38.960
It's called break glass.

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:41.740
So again, deployer comes along
and says, production's on fire.

00:17:41.740 --> 00:17:43.940
I have this quick fix here.

00:17:43.940 --> 00:17:45.270
Let me just push it through.

00:17:45.270 --> 00:17:49.190
I will specify break glass
flag, and the request

00:17:49.190 --> 00:17:51.690
will reach Kubernetes
as usual, go

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:53.890
through the whole
verification process.

00:17:53.890 --> 00:17:56.800
Binary authorization says,
no, this is not conformant.

00:17:56.800 --> 00:17:59.090
But Kubernetes
master will say, I'm

00:17:59.090 --> 00:18:03.960
overriding it because break
glass flag is turned on.

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:05.900
And this will
generate an audit log

00:18:05.900 --> 00:18:13.660
so the break glass deployment
event can be reviewed later on.

00:18:13.660 --> 00:18:17.062
So this is binary authorization,
coming soon to beta.

00:18:17.062 --> 00:18:18.270
We just announced beta today.

00:18:18.270 --> 00:18:21.930
The code will be
available shortly.

00:18:21.930 --> 00:18:26.590
Hopefully, I've convinced you
that code-based deployment

00:18:26.590 --> 00:18:31.570
control is more secure than
account-based deployment

00:18:31.570 --> 00:18:33.880
control, and binary
authorization

00:18:33.880 --> 00:18:38.848
would provide that for
a Kubernetes engine.

00:18:38.848 --> 00:18:43.420
We will support
policies associated

00:18:43.420 --> 00:18:47.140
with a runtime environment, at
both project and cluster level,

00:18:47.140 --> 00:18:52.920
so you can define enforcement
at different granularities.

00:18:52.920 --> 00:18:57.010
We'll have integration with GCR
Vulnerability Scanner, as well

00:18:57.010 --> 00:18:59.020
as the Cloud
Container Builder, so

00:18:59.020 --> 00:19:02.950
that you can apply deploy
policies based on vulnerability

00:19:02.950 --> 00:19:06.440
findings and build information.

00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:10.430
We'll support whitelisting
trusted third party images

00:19:10.430 --> 00:19:13.960
so you can have standards
across your organization

00:19:13.960 --> 00:19:17.460
on what third party
images to run.

00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:20.760
In case of emergency,
break glass.

00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:23.160
We'll integrate it with
IAM and Audit Logging

00:19:23.160 --> 00:19:26.730
so that you can review
failed attempts, brake glass

00:19:26.730 --> 00:19:30.750
deployment events later
on, after the fact,

00:19:30.750 --> 00:19:33.460
We also want to make it really
easy for you to integrate this

00:19:33.460 --> 00:19:35.800
into your CI/CD pipeline
and make it easier

00:19:35.800 --> 00:19:37.730
for you to write
binary authorization

00:19:37.730 --> 00:19:39.890
signatures for an image.

00:19:39.890 --> 00:19:42.510
So we'll have partner support
from popular security tools

00:19:42.510 --> 00:19:44.890
such as Twistlock,
popular CI/CD tools,

00:19:44.890 --> 00:19:48.880
such as CloudBees and Jenkins,
to make it easy for you.

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:52.600
Last, but not least, we
have open source reference

00:19:52.600 --> 00:19:55.150
implementation for
Grafeas and Kritis

00:19:55.150 --> 00:19:59.080
so you have the building block
to implement similar security

00:19:59.080 --> 00:20:04.130
controls for your
on-prem deployment.

00:20:04.130 --> 00:20:06.320
All right, so that
concludes the overview

00:20:06.320 --> 00:20:08.580
for binary authorization,
and hopefully I

00:20:08.580 --> 00:20:13.490
gave you a taste of how to
secure your software supply

00:20:13.490 --> 00:20:16.700
chain using these new
products that we've announced.

00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:19.910
Next to come, we'll hand
the floor to Jonathan.

00:20:19.910 --> 00:20:23.870
Jonathan will show you guys a
demo on binary authorization

00:20:23.870 --> 00:20:27.890
work in action, and combine it
with other security controls

00:20:27.890 --> 00:20:30.720
to secure Shopify's
production environment.

