because it contained
vulnerabilities,

00:27:27.430 --> 00:27:29.555
and we don't want any
vulnerabilities in the images

00:27:29.555 --> 00:27:31.270
that we deploy today.

00:27:31.270 --> 00:27:37.269
So earlier we talked
about this policy.

00:27:37.269 --> 00:27:39.060
So we're going to take
a look at the policy

00:27:39.060 --> 00:27:42.070
that we have set up for the
clusters in our project.

00:27:42.070 --> 00:27:45.060
So this policy, for
binary authorization,

00:27:45.060 --> 00:27:48.420
contains some whitelisting,
because if we didn't whitelist

00:27:48.420 --> 00:27:51.850
these three repositories,
my Kubernetes cluster

00:27:51.850 --> 00:27:53.640
wouldn't start on GKE.

00:27:53.640 --> 00:27:56.210
So what I've just done
is just globbed every--

00:27:56.210 --> 00:27:58.470
every image inside of
those GCR repositories

00:27:58.470 --> 00:28:01.630
has been listed for the
purposes of the demo.

00:28:01.630 --> 00:28:03.960
Please do not do this at home.

00:28:03.960 --> 00:28:06.169
OK, so I'm going to run
through the rest of this.

00:28:06.169 --> 00:28:07.710
We have some cluster
admission rules.

00:28:07.710 --> 00:28:11.966
So earlier, we said that we can
specify a policy on a project

00:28:11.966 --> 00:28:13.590
level, and on a
cluster level, so we're

00:28:13.590 --> 00:28:15.414
going to try to demo
both of those today.

00:28:15.414 --> 00:28:16.830
So in the cluster
admission rules,

00:28:16.830 --> 00:28:20.905
I have running in Canada, or
North America Northeast one,

00:28:20.905 --> 00:28:24.711
a test cluster an evaluation
mode require attestation.

00:28:24.711 --> 00:28:26.460
So what this means,
is that any image that

00:28:26.460 --> 00:28:28.084
deploys a binary
authorization is going

00:28:28.084 --> 00:28:29.910
to match these attestations.

00:28:29.910 --> 00:28:33.615
And you can see that they're
named Provenance and Snake Oil,

00:28:33.615 --> 00:28:34.699
just like we talked about.

00:28:34.699 --> 00:28:36.823
For all the other clusters
running in this project,

00:28:36.823 --> 00:28:38.490
we only want to
make sure that it's

00:28:38.490 --> 00:28:41.400
built by a trusted identity,
and that the image actually

00:28:41.400 --> 00:28:42.370
was validated.

00:28:42.370 --> 00:28:46.610
So, moment of truth, we're going
to try to deploy some images.

00:28:46.610 --> 00:28:50.600
We're going to grab our
good sha that passed,

00:28:50.600 --> 00:28:57.254
and we're going to keep
CTL, run good times,

00:28:57.254 --> 00:29:00.050
and we're going to
try to run the thing.

00:29:00.050 --> 00:29:02.862
So we're going to do also--

00:29:02.862 --> 00:29:03.824
come on.

00:29:06.710 --> 00:29:07.250
Come on.

00:29:07.250 --> 00:29:09.440
Hey, look at that.

00:29:09.440 --> 00:29:13.040
Then we're also going to grab
the bad sha, the one that

00:29:13.040 --> 00:29:18.804
failed, and we're going to
keep CTL, run bad times.

00:29:18.804 --> 00:29:20.850
We're going to deploy those.

00:29:20.850 --> 00:29:24.630
So what do we all
expect would happen?

00:29:24.630 --> 00:29:27.300
Well, I would certainly
expect, based on my policy,

00:29:27.300 --> 00:29:30.330
to have one good times
deployment, and no bad times

00:29:30.330 --> 00:29:30.910
deployment.

00:29:30.910 --> 00:29:33.930
So let's check it out,
and see what happened.

00:29:33.930 --> 00:29:36.380
Starting to get some
pods, and sure enough,

00:29:36.380 --> 00:29:38.330
only one good times
pod is running,

00:29:38.330 --> 00:29:39.971
because both
attestations have been

00:29:39.971 --> 00:29:42.470
created based on the policy
that we created for our project,

00:29:42.470 --> 00:29:45.650
and in this specific case,
our clustered mission rules.

00:29:45.650 --> 00:29:49.760
So, cool, but what does
that mean for a developer?

00:29:49.760 --> 00:29:52.580
How can we get any sort
of actionable feedback?

00:29:52.580 --> 00:29:53.930
Where does this live?

00:29:53.930 --> 00:29:55.460
How did that fail?

00:29:55.460 --> 00:29:57.620
I'm a command line
person, so I'm

00:29:57.620 --> 00:30:00.700
going to get the replica sets
that have been created here.

00:30:00.700 --> 00:30:03.300
A deployment in Kubernetes
is like the highest level

00:30:03.300 --> 00:30:05.180
abstraction, which
creates replica sets,

00:30:05.180 --> 00:30:07.081
and further to
that, creates pods.

00:30:07.081 --> 00:30:09.080
So we've created the
deployment object and said,

00:30:09.080 --> 00:30:10.760
hey, cool, we want
this container to run.

00:30:10.760 --> 00:30:12.260
And a created replica
set said, hey, cool,

00:30:12.260 --> 00:30:14.240
we're going to try to
start this pod for you.

00:30:14.240 --> 00:30:17.920
You can see the bad times
has one container desired,

00:30:17.920 --> 00:30:18.870
but none are ready.

00:30:18.870 --> 00:30:20.786
So we're going to see
what happened with that.

00:30:26.210 --> 00:30:26.790
Boom.

00:30:26.790 --> 00:30:28.704
Talk about error messaging.

00:30:28.704 --> 00:30:30.870
For those who don't know,
Kubernetes controllers are

00:30:30.870 --> 00:30:33.480
on like a TL;DR for loop, so
it's just going to keep trying

00:30:33.480 --> 00:30:35.670
to do this thing over,
and over, and over again.

00:30:35.670 --> 00:30:39.070
And every time that it tries, we
have an error creating the pod.

00:30:39.070 --> 00:30:40.710
It's forbidden because it's--

00:30:40.710 --> 00:30:43.771
an image policy webhook backend
denied one or more images,

00:30:43.771 --> 00:30:44.270
right?

00:30:44.270 --> 00:30:45.360
So every time that
it gets deployed,

00:30:45.360 --> 00:30:47.850
there's some shenanigans and
webhooks that fire around,

00:30:47.850 --> 00:30:49.420
that help this do its thing.

00:30:49.420 --> 00:30:51.420
It was denied by the
attestation authority.

00:30:51.420 --> 00:30:55.350
We cannot find any Snake Oil
attestations for this image,

00:30:55.350 --> 00:30:56.950
such that it failed.

00:30:56.950 --> 00:30:57.450
Neat.

00:31:00.820 --> 00:31:03.340
What about on all
the other clusters?

00:31:03.340 --> 00:31:05.340
Just to prove that
the policy works,

00:31:05.340 --> 00:31:07.201
I created a yellow
cluster in here.

00:31:07.201 --> 00:31:09.700
And what I'm going to do is,
I'm going to try to run the bad

00:31:09.700 --> 00:31:13.450
times image-- the same one
that was attested earlier with

00:31:13.450 --> 00:31:15.970
Voucher--

00:31:15.970 --> 00:31:20.240
and, fingers crossed, it should
deploy to my yellow cluster.

00:31:20.240 --> 00:31:21.180
Looks like it.

00:31:24.470 --> 00:31:26.500
Then we have one bad
times pod running.

00:31:26.500 --> 00:31:29.790
So this sort of proves that the
policy is working as intended.

00:31:29.790 --> 00:31:34.080
Again, I'm just going to bring
up that policy to show you

00:31:34.080 --> 00:31:35.160
what that looks like.

00:31:35.160 --> 00:31:38.290
Everything's YAML these
days, such as the policy,

00:31:38.290 --> 00:31:40.020
and so it's pretty easy to read.

00:31:40.020 --> 00:31:42.690
It should be easy to read
for, not just security people,

00:31:42.690 --> 00:31:46.110
but for other engineers and
operations people alike.

00:31:46.110 --> 00:31:48.510
It's important that
technologies like this

00:31:48.510 --> 00:31:50.970
are our human-readable
and easy to digest,

00:31:50.970 --> 00:31:53.850
or else we can't
implement them properly.

00:31:53.850 --> 00:31:59.200
So further into that, BinAuth is
not just a command line suite.

00:31:59.200 --> 00:32:02.910
It does come with a fancy GUI
now, thanks to our UX folks

00:32:02.910 --> 00:32:05.800
over here, who made this
the way it is today.

00:32:05.800 --> 00:32:09.900
So I put some human readable
descriptions on our signatures,

00:32:09.900 --> 00:32:14.010
or our testers, just to say
what this is actually doing,

00:32:14.010 --> 00:32:16.440
such that you know if I
name something willy-nilly

00:32:16.440 --> 00:32:19.710
like Snake Oil, that doesn't
mean anything to anybody,

00:32:19.710 --> 00:32:21.570
