There are other
types of products

00:38:39.740 --> 00:38:43.280
out there, maybe like
Docker Content Trust, things

00:38:43.280 --> 00:38:46.730
like that, that also
involves image signing,

00:38:46.730 --> 00:38:49.940
but that's more around
the software distribution

00:38:49.940 --> 00:38:57.680
protection versus a
Provenance enforcement.

00:38:57.680 --> 00:39:03.060
So the difference there is,
while softwares are distributed

00:39:03.060 --> 00:39:06.150
in an open internet
environment, it

00:39:06.150 --> 00:39:11.410
may get altered while its moved
from repository to repository,

00:39:11.410 --> 00:39:13.800
and signatures, such as
Docker Content Trust,

00:39:13.800 --> 00:39:16.570
makes sure that the software
has not been altered--

00:39:16.570 --> 00:39:18.630
it comes from a trusted source.

00:39:18.630 --> 00:39:22.350
But Binary Authorization takes
a slightly different approach,

00:39:22.350 --> 00:39:25.380
focusing on largely
collaborative environments,

00:39:25.380 --> 00:39:27.030
which is enterprise
organization,

00:39:27.030 --> 00:39:29.280
and you want to make sure
that the software you deploy

00:39:29.280 --> 00:39:31.830
has passed certain
tests that are

00:39:31.830 --> 00:39:34.509
hosted by you-- that
you have control over.

00:39:34.509 --> 00:39:35.050
AUDIENCE: OK.

